---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Never load the whole folder — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="A folder of 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB of memory and crashed the app. The fix is the idea I reuse most in the code. I made the same mistake four times."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-09-01">September 1, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Never load the whole folder</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          A folder of 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB of memory, and then the app
          crashed. The fix is the idea I reuse most in the code. I made the
          same mistake four times, each time in a different part of the app.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 4 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          LightPhotos crashed on 8,889 photos because I loaded a thumbnail for
          every photo. Now the app keeps only what is on screen, plus a few
          extra. Then I found the same mistake three more times.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="folder-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="all" aria-pressed="true">Load every photo</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="screen" aria-pressed="false">Only what is on screen</button>
          <label>Photos <input type="range" min="0" max="7" step="1" value="5" aria-valuetext="8,889 photos"></label>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 236" role="img" aria-label="Chart of memory against the number of photos in a folder. If the app loads every photo, memory increases to 1.9 GB at 8,889 photos, and then the app crashes. If the app loads only what is on screen, memory stays at 110 to 150 MB for any folder size.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">MEMORY AS THE FOLDER GROWS</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="60" y1="30" x2="60" y2="200"></line>
          <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="60" y1="200" x2="620" y2="200"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="52" y="52" text-anchor="end">1.9 GB</text>
          <line class="lp-fig-line-soft" x1="60" y1="48" x2="620" y2="48"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="52" y="204" text-anchor="end">0</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="60" y="218" text-anchor="middle">0</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="309" y="218" text-anchor="middle">8,889</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="620" y="218" text-anchor="end">20,000</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="620" y="232" text-anchor="end">photos in the folder</text>

          <g data-line="all">
            <line class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x1="60" y1="200" x2="309" y2="48" stroke-width="2"></line>
            <path class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" stroke-width="2" d="M302 41 L316 55 M316 41 L302 55"></path>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="330" y="44">Load every photo</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="330" y="61">1.9 GB, then crash</text>
          </g>
          <g data-line="screen" opacity="0.45">
            <rect class="lp-fig-bar" x="60" y="188" width="560" height="3"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="620" y="166" text-anchor="end">Only what is on screen</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="620" y="182" text-anchor="end">110 to 150 MB at any size</text>
          </g>

          <line class="lp-fig-line" data-cursor x1="309" y1="30" x2="309" y2="200" stroke-dasharray="3 3"></line>
          <circle class="lp-fig-bad" data-dot cx="309" cy="48" r="5"></circle>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">8,889 photos, every photo loaded: 8,889 textures, 1.3 GB of video memory, 1.9 GB in total. Then the OS stops the app.</p>
        <figcaption>
          Drag the folder size, then switch the method. The flat line depends
          on the size of the window, not on the number of photos.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>What I learned</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>
          <strong>Look for loops over everything the user gave you.</strong>
          They grow with the number of photos a person owns.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Size memory by what you must keep at the same time,</strong>
          not by the number of photos in a job.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Write the calculation next to the number.</strong> A person
          who later reads only "32" must know what can make it wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Check old numbers again when things change.</strong> Three
          of these four bugs were correct when I wrote them.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>The crash</h2>
      <p>
        A wedding photographer gives you a card with 8,889 photos. You open the
        folder in LightPhotos, and the app crashes. My grid code did
        approximately this:
      </p>
      <pre class="lp-code">for each photo in folder:
    request a thumbnail
    upload it to the GPU as a texture</pre>
      <p>
        That loop is fine for twenty photos. With 8,889 photos, the textures
        use about 1.3 GB of video memory, the app uses about 1.9 GB, and the
        operating system stops it. The loop assumes that a folder is small.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the first time</h2>
      <p>
        A large monitor shows about forty thumbnails. Nobody needs the other
        8,849 yet. Now the grid draws only the rows that you scrolled to, plus
        a few extra rows so that scrolling stays smooth. Web developers call
        this list virtualisation.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the second time</h2>
      <p>
        The thumbnail cache has a fixed maximum count. I picked it when
        thumbnails were small. Later, thumbnails became 512 pixels on the long
        side and about 700 KB each, roughly seven times the old cost. Nothing
        crashed, but the limit no longer meant what I intended.
      </p>
      <p>
        So I calculated it again from what must be in memory at the same
        time: the visible grid plus three extra rows above and below, about
        180 cells. The limit is now 256. A lower limit makes the cache discard
        thumbnails right after it loads them, which is worse than no cache.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the third time</h2>
      <p>
        Auto Tone analyses each selected photo. My first version requested
        every thumbnail at the start. With 20,000 photos at roughly 0.4 MB
        each, that is about 8 GB. The browser tab has a limit of 4 GB, and it
        never gives memory back.
      </p>

      <figure class="lp-figure lp-try" id="tone-demo">
        <div class="lp-try-controls">
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="all" aria-pressed="false">Request all at start</button>
          <button type="button" class="lp-try-btn" data-mode="window" aria-pressed="true">Window of 32</button>
          <label>Selected <input type="range" min="0" max="5" step="1" value="4" aria-valuetext="20,000 photos"></label>
        </div>
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 214" role="img" aria-label="A window of 32 photos moves across a selection of 20,000 photos and uses about 13 MB. If the app requests every thumbnail at the start, memory is about 8 GB, which is above the 4 GB limit of the browser tab.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">AUTO TONE ACROSS A SELECTION</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="0" y="30" width="640" height="38" rx="6"></rect>
          <g data-view="window">
            <rect class="lp-fig-bar-dim" x="1" y="31" width="199" height="36"></rect>
            <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="200" y="24" width="76" height="50" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-t" x="238" y="54" text-anchor="middle">32</text>
            <line class="lp-fig-line" x1="288" y1="49" x2="332" y2="49"></line>
            <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M332 43 L332 55 L344 49 Z"></path>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="100" y="90" text-anchor="middle">already analysed</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="238" y="90" text-anchor="middle">in memory</text>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="460" y="90" text-anchor="middle">not requested yet</text>
          </g>
          <g data-view="all" display="none">
            <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="0" y="30" width="640" height="38" rx="6"></rect>
            <text class="lp-fig-s" x="320" y="90" text-anchor="middle">every thumbnail requested at the start</text>
          </g>

          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="122">memory</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box" x="60" y="130" width="560" height="22" rx="3"></rect>
          <rect class="lp-fig-bar" data-bar x="60" y="130" width="2" height="22" rx="2"></rect>
          <line class="lp-fig-bad-stroke" x1="247" y1="122" x2="247" y2="160" stroke-width="2"></line>
          <text class="lp-fig-s lp-fig-bad" x="253" y="122">tab limit 4 GB</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="60" y="170" text-anchor="middle">0</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="620" y="170" text-anchor="middle">12 GB</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="0" y="204">Slowest cold RAW decode 137 ms &divide; one analysis 8 ms = 17, rounded up to 32.</text>
        </svg>
        <p class="lp-try-readout" aria-live="polite">20,000 selected, window of 32: about 13 MB.</p>
        <figcaption>
          The window uses about 13 MB for 30 selected photos or for 30,000. A
          check in the code stops the build if the window is 64 or above, so
          nobody can turn it into a casual setting.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>
        The analysis of one photo takes about 8 ms on the main thread, so I
        cannot split it across CPU cores. A thumbnail from an uncached RAW
        file takes 137 ms in the slow cases (95 out of 100 loads are faster).
        The loading must stay ahead of the analysis even then, so the window
        is the slowest load divided by one analysis.
      </p>
      <p>
        I assumed that the loads made the batch slow. I was wrong. The
        analysis takes about fifty-five times longer, because it cannot run in
        parallel. Faster loading would not help.
      </p>

      <h2>The fix, the fourth time</h2>
      <p>
        The large view loads a photo at a size that fits the window, so a
        one-pixel resize can mean a new load. Now the size rounds up to a
        multiple of 512 pixels. A drag of the window edge causes only a few
        reloads, not one for every frame. It is the same idea, applied to a
        value that changes smoothly: if an expensive cache depends on it, I
        round it.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/six-queues-and-a-reserved-worker.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Six queues and a reserved worker</span>
        </a>
        <a class="lp-article-nav-next" href="/blogs/measuring-a-gui-app.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Next</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">Measuring a GUI app without lying to yourself</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>

  <script>
    const fmt = (n) => n.toLocaleString("en-US");

    // Wire a pair of toggle buttons and a stepped range to one render function.
    function wire(fig, stops, initialMode, render) {
      const btns = fig.querySelectorAll("[data-mode]");
      const range = fig.querySelector('input[type="range"]');
      let mode = initialMode;
      const update = () => {
        const n = stops[Number(range.value)];
        range.setAttribute("aria-valuetext", `${fmt(n)} photos`);
        btns.forEach((b) => b.setAttribute("aria-pressed", String(b.dataset.mode === mode)));
        render(mode, n);
      };
      btns.forEach((b) => b.addEventListener("click", () => { mode = b.dataset.mode; update(); }));
      range.addEventListener("input", update);
    }

    // Grid demo. Facts from the post: 8,889 photos used 1.9 GB (1.3 GB of
    // textures), then the app crashed; the virtualised grid uses 110 to 150 MB
    // and keeps about 180 cells (visible grid plus three rows each side).
    const folder = document.getElementById("folder-demo");
    if (folder) {
      const CRASH = 8889;
      const x = (n) => 60 + (560 * n) / 20000;
      const yAll = (n) => 200 - (152 * n) / CRASH;
      const cursor = folder.querySelector("[data-cursor]");
      const dot = folder.querySelector("[data-dot]");
      const readout = folder.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      wire(folder, [20, 500, 2000, 4000, 6000, 8889, 15000, 20000], "all", (mode, n) => {
        folder.querySelector('[data-line="all"]').setAttribute("opacity", mode === "all" ? "1" : "0.45");
        folder.querySelector('[data-line="screen"]').setAttribute("opacity", mode === "screen" ? "1" : "0.45");
        const cx = mode === "all" ? x(Math.min(n, CRASH)) : x(n);
        cursor.setAttribute("x1", x(n));
        cursor.setAttribute("x2", x(n));
        dot.setAttribute("cx", cx);
        dot.setAttribute("cy", mode === "all" ? yAll(Math.min(n, CRASH)) : 189);
        dot.setAttribute("class", mode === "all" ? "lp-fig-bad" : "lp-fig-bar");
        let text;
        if (mode === "screen") {
          text = `${fmt(n)} photos, only what is on screen: ${fmt(Math.min(n, 180))} thumbnails in memory. 110 to 150 MB.`;
        } else if (n < CRASH) {
          text = `${fmt(n)} photos, every photo loaded: ${fmt(n)} textures. Memory grows with every photo.`;
        } else if (n === CRASH) {
          text = "8,889 photos, every photo loaded: 8,889 textures, 1.3 GB of video memory, 1.9 GB in total. Then the OS stops the app.";
        } else {
          text = `${fmt(n)} photos, every photo loaded: the app crashed at 8,889.`;
        }
        readout.textContent = text;
      });
    }

    // Auto Tone demo. Facts from the post: roughly 0.4 MB per thumbnail,
    // a 4 GB tab limit, and about 13 MB for the window of 32.
    const tone = document.getElementById("tone-demo");
    if (tone) {
      const bar = tone.querySelector("[data-bar]");
      const readout = tone.querySelector(".lp-try-readout");
      const MAX_MB = 12000;
      const size = (mb) => (mb >= 1000 ? `about ${fmt(Math.round(mb / 100) / 10)} GB` : `about ${fmt(Math.round(mb))} MB`);
      wire(tone, [30, 300, 3000, 10000, 20000, 30000], "window", (mode, n) => {
        const mb = mode === "all" ? n * 0.4 : 13;
        tone.querySelector('[data-view="all"]').setAttribute("display", mode === "all" ? "inline" : "none");
        tone.querySelector('[data-view="window"]').setAttribute("display", mode === "window" ? "inline" : "none");
        bar.setAttribute("width", Math.max(2, (560 * Math.min(mb, MAX_MB)) / MAX_MB));
        bar.setAttribute("class", mb > 4000 ? "lp-fig-bad" : "lp-fig-bar");
        const over = mb > 4000 ? " Above the 4 GB tab limit." : "";
        readout.textContent = mode === "all"
          ? `${fmt(n)} selected, all at start: ${size(mb)}.${over}`
          : `${fmt(n)} selected, window of 32: about 13 MB.`;
      });
    }
  </script>
</Base>
